# Atlas UI Rules

Design direction: dark-first, high-taste, consumer-grade SaaS.
Atlas UI must feel like a quiet editorial operating desk, not a card dashboard.

The current static prototype is rejected.
Do not use `docs/prototypes/atlas-ui-system/index.html` as accepted visual direction.
Use `docs/architecture/ui-quality-bar.md` before any new high-fidelity UI work.
Read `docs/design/atlas-ui-reference-audit.md` and `docs/design/atlas-ui-directions.md` before implementing UI.

Layout precision is mandatory.
Do not ship a design or frontend change without explicit margin, padding, height, width, wrapping, and overflow behavior.

Default layout measurements:

- Product shell: `100vh` minimum height.
- App left navigation: `216px` desktop, `190px` tablet.
- Right inspector: `326px` desktop, hidden below `1160px`.
- Topline: `72px` minimum height.
- Page padding: `26px` desktop, `16px` mobile.
- Content padding: `24px` desktop, `16px` mobile.
- Inspector padding: `22px 18px`.
- Button height: `36px` minimum.
- Navigation row height: `32px` minimum.
- Data row height: `54px` minimum.
- Composer input height: `42px` minimum.
- Graph canvas height: `600px` minimum desktop.

Overflow rules:

- Use `min-width: 0` on text-bearing grid/flex children.
- Use `overflow-wrap: anywhere` for object titles, URLs, file names, source names, and email subjects.
- Do not use `<br>` for layout.
- Main content and inspector scroll independently.
- Row state text may stay on one line on desktop, but must wrap on mobile.
- Any overlap, clipped label, accidental horizontal scroll, or hidden source/risk state is a product bug.

Avoid nested cards, decorative pills, generic gradients, noisy animations, excessive icons, and repeated attention markers.

Required surfaces:

- Today dashboard
- Capture inbox
- Chat
- Search
- Graph
- Object inspector
- Documents
- People
- Projects
- Shared spaces
- Privacy center

Every important AI or memory state must show provenance, confidence, visibility, and correction path when relevant.

Use Storybook as the source-of-truth surface for shared UI components, tokens, states, and mock screens once the app scaffold exists.
Paper, Pencil, Figma, or standalone HTML explorations are references only until translated into code-backed stories and route mockups.
